// **The piece tier of a broken tree**, as against `deadwood`'s grit tier.
//
// `wood` had one debris target and it was a `Powder`, so every outcome of a
// felled tree — a whole bole, a major limb, a twig — ended as grains that
// pile at `deadwood`'s 38-degree angle of repose. Two thousand seven hundred
// of them make a cone of sawdust, and that is literally the same shape
// `ascii`'s "angle of repose" scene draws on purpose. Reported from play as
// *"it reads as a tree disintegrating into dust"*; the full account is
// `Reports/physical-trees-design-2026-08-23.md` §5.5.
//
// So the fix is a second target rather than a tuned first one: `wood`
// `severs_into` this and `breaks_into` deadwood, and which one a cell gets
// is decided by how big the piece it belonged to was. A log lies where it
// fell; grit piles. The physics genuinely differ on numbers that already
// exist -- it does not flow, it can be stood on, it burns and rots on its
// own schedule -- which is `Reports/design-philosophy.md` §2a's test for
// when a constant becomes a material rather than a knob.
//
// `Solid`, not `Plant`: it is dead. Nothing grows from it, `plant.rs` never
// walks it, and `decay.rs` is free to take it back to soil in its own time.
(
    name: "log",
    display: "Log",
    kind: Solid,
    // Stand on it and climb it, which is half of why this is worth having
    // as its own material: a felled trunk lying across a gap is terrain the
    // gnome can use, and a heap of powder is not.
    climbable: true,
    // Under living `wood`'s 0.9 -- seasoned timber has lost its water. Above
    // `deadwood`'s 0.7, since that is loose comminuted debris with air in it
    // and this is not.
    density: 0.8,
    // Paler and greyer than living bark (wood.ron's band 0 starts at
    // 92,64,40) and than `deadwood` (74,50,30), and deliberately the
    // *lightest* of the three. The acceptance question for the whole
    // package is whether a settled fall reads as logs lying on the ground
    // rather than as a mound, and that is judged by eye at play zoom -- a
    // piece tier that draws the same colour as the grit it is lying in
    // cannot be told from it however well the simulation is behaving.
    //
    // **The four are deliberately close together, where every other debris
    // material's four are deliberately far apart.** Palette spread is what
    // makes a powder read as a powder: `deadwood` runs (64,43,26) to
    // (96,66,40) and `litter` (132,96,44) to (182,146,78), and a field of
    // cells drawing randomly across that range is *speckle*, which is
    // exactly right for grains and exactly wrong for a piece. A log is one
    // object, so its cells must draw nearly the same colour and render as a
    // smooth block -- the shape of the fragment is the thing the eye has to
    // be able to find in the pile, and a 44-unit spread hid it inside the
    // grit it landed in. Judged by eye at play zoom on the settled
    // `scene=fell` pile, which is the only way this could have been judged.
    // **Warm timber, not grey.** The first shipped palette was a
    // desaturated warm grey, chosen so a piece would stand out against
    // `litter`'s gold and `deadwood`'s dark brown -- and it did stand out,
    // as the wrong thing. Watching the fall frame by frame the owner's
    // words for it were *"the trunk of the tree doesn't move it just turns
    // gray"*: a bole promoted, landing on its own stump with nowhere to go
    // and re-rendering in place, read as tissue **dying** rather than as a
    // log. Grey is what ash and stone are here.
    //
    // So: pale, warm, and light enough to separate from everything else in
    // a debris pile without borrowing gold from `litter` (132,96,44 to
    // 182,146,78) or dark from `deadwood` (64,43,26 to 96,66,40). Cut
    // timber is the reference, not bark -- this is the inside of a broken
    // trunk.
    // Landed at pale cream-timber after two rejected attempts, and both
    // rejections are worth keeping because they are the two ways this can
    // fail. A desaturated **grey** was legible in the pile and read as the
    // wood *dying* -- the owner's word, watching the fall, was "turns
    // gray". A warm mid-**brown** read as wood and vanished into `litter`'s
    // gold, which is the same hue family at nearly the same value. What is
    // left is the axis neither used: go **light**, and pinker than gold, so
    // it separates on value from `deadwood` and on hue from `litter` while
    // still being the colour of the inside of a broken trunk.
    // **`wood.ron`'s palette, copied entry for entry.** Owner, after two
    // wrong readings of the same complaint: *"The color should not change at
    // all"*, and then *"and the trunks and branches shouldn't change colour
    // either."*
    //
    // Everything this block used to argue was about telling a *piece* from
    // the *grit* it lies in, and that argument still holds -- it just never
    // needed a new hue to make it. A felled trunk is the colour it was
    // standing; it greys off later, by decaying, not at the instant it stops
    // moving. `dead-ends.md` carries three attempts to find the "right" log
    // colour (a wide spread that read as speckle, a grey that read as tissue
    // dying, a warm brown that vanished into litter) and a fourth of mine
    // that gave every felled tree birch-grey bark whatever species it was.
    // Four failures on one axis is the signal the axis was wrong: **the
    // answer is not a colour, it is that there is no change.**
    //
    // Carried index for index by `rigid::settle`, which copies the cell's own
    // shade across whenever the two palettes are the same length. So a
    // felled pine lies there pine-coloured and a felled birch birch-coloured
    // -- a `wood` band is the individual's *bark density*, chosen by its own
    // genome (`organism::bark_band_for_density`), so preserving the index is
    // what keeps a stand's variation on the ground instead of flattening it
    // to one tone.
    //
    // Still distinct from what it lies among, which was the real requirement:
    // `deadwood` (64,43,26 to 96,66,40) and `litter` (132,96,44 to
    // 182,146,78) are unchanged and neither is in this range.
    colors: [
        // 0: mid brown — the original palette
        (92, 64, 40),
        (105, 74, 46),
        (80, 55, 34),
        (114, 81, 51),
        // 1: dark red-brown — the dense end of `tree` (conifer moved to 4-5)
        (78, 48, 34),
        (92, 58, 42),
        (64, 38, 26),
        (104, 68, 50),
        // 2: near-black brown — old or wet bark
        (58, 42, 30),
        (70, 52, 38),
        (46, 33, 23),
        (82, 62, 46),
        // 3: grey-brown — birch, and most shrub stems
        (112, 100, 84),
        (128, 116, 98),
        (96, 85, 70),
        (142, 130, 112),
        // 4: rust — a pine's warm, plated bark
        (124, 76, 48),
        (142, 90, 60),
        (106, 62, 38),
        (158, 106, 74),
        // 5: plum-brown — the dense end of the same, spruce rather than pine
        (92, 60, 52),
        (106, 72, 62),
        (76, 48, 42),
        (120, 86, 76),
        // 6: olive-drab — a green stem that has only just gone woody
        (96, 92, 62),
        (110, 106, 74),
        (80, 76, 50),
        (124, 120, 88),
        // 7: pale green-grey — an old vine stem
        (118, 118, 102),
        (134, 134, 118),
        (100, 100, 86),
        (150, 150, 134),
    ],
    // Between living wood's 0.25 and deadwood's 0.35: drier than the tree it
    // came off, less finely divided than the grit. Same burn temperature and
    // duration as `wood` -- a log burns for a while, which is exactly the
    // number wood.ron already carries and this is the same timber.
    flammability: 0.3,
    ignition_temperature: 400.0,
    burn_temperature: 700.0,
    burn_duration: 420,
    burns_into: "ash",
    heat_conductivity: 0.15,
    // The grit tier: hit a log hard enough, or blast it, and it comes apart
    // into the same debris a snapped twig makes.
    // **A fallen log is not ground for a living plant.** Terrain is; this
    // is the tree's own wreckage. Without this, a chip knocked off a bole
    // lands beside the stump and anchors the crown that the cut just
    // detached -- see `MaterialDef::anchors_organisms` for the measurement.
    anchors_organisms: false,
    breaks_into: "deadwood",
    // **A log that gives way breaks into smaller logs, not into sawdust.**
    // Same two fields `wood.ron` carries and the same argument: this is a
    // piece, so its own ladder has to start where a piece is possible, or
    // the first time a settling heap shifts underneath it the whole tier
    // falls back through `MIN_BODY_CELLS` into grit. Measured on
    // `scene=fell fell=7150` at frame 8,750, everything else held: log
    // standing 569 -> **624** cells and deadwood 505 -> 396.
    //
    // `woody` here is about the flood, not about severance -- a landed log
    // has no organism id, so `fell_severed_tissue` never sees it. Eight is
    // right for the same reason it is right for the wood this came off:
    // a jumbled pile of pieces is connected the way it was placed.
    woody: true,
    fragment_floor: 5,
    // Rots, slowly. `litter` runs 0.5 damp / 0.1 dry because a leaf is thin;
    // a log is not, and a forest floor that ate its own deadfall inside a
    // few hundred frames would undo the thing this material exists to show.
    decays_into: "soil",
    decay_chance_damp: 0.02,
    decay_chance_dry: 0.002,
    // **No `max_unsupported_span`, so it is opted out of the structural
    // check** (the field defaults to the u16::MAX "never" sentinel). That is
    // deliberate and it is the point of the tier: a log that landed is
    // finished moving. Giving it a finite span puts a settled fall back on
    // the failure path, where a piece resting on its own rubble is judged
    // as a knife-edge footing and comes apart into deadwood -- the dust
    // outcome, arriving one frame later by a different route. If a stack of
    // logs ever needs to be able to collapse, that is a span *and* a bearing
    // rule that knows it is looking at a piece rather than a cell
    // (`CLAUDE.md`, "which object does this rule evaluate"), and it is not
    // this package.
)
